; wlvfsd: an SELinux domain for the virtiofsd sidecar of a VM workload.
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS
;
; Once a VM workload's QEMU is confined as svirt_t (ADR 006 step 3), virtiofs
; volumes break, because SELinux checks `connectto` against the *peer process's
; domain*, not the socket file's label:
;
;   denied { connectto } path="/run/workload-vm/<name>/virtiofs-t0.sock"
;       scontext=svirt_t tcontext=unconfined_service_t tclass=unix_stream_socket
;
; Fedora 44 ships no virtiofsd domain at all — no virtiofsd_t, no
; virtiofsd_exec_t, no fcontext entry; /usr/libexec/virtiofsd is plain bin_t.
; (virtiofs_t exists, but that is the *filesystem* type.) So systemd execs our
; sidecar into unconfined_service_t, and svirt_t may not connect to it.
;
; This is not an upstream gap waiting to be filled. libvirt never labels
; virtiofsd: eight of its QEMU helpers get a child-process label through
; qemuSecurityCommandRun, and qemu_virtiofs.c is the one that does not — it
; launches with a plain virCommandRun, so the daemon inherits its parent's
; domain. Under system-mode libvirt that parent is virtqemud_t, which is in the
; virt_driver_domain attribute that svirt_t may already connect to; system
; libvirt works by inheritance rather than by design. A bare systemd sidecar has
; no such parent. libvirt has no virtiofsd domain because inheritance covers its
; cases, and the policy has none because libvirt never asked.
;
; WHY A DOMAIN RATHER THAN THE BLANKET ALLOW
;
; The cheap fix is one rule:
;
;   allow svirt_t unconfined_service_t:unix_stream_socket connectto;
;
; and it has distro precedent — selinux-policy-40.26-1.fc40 shipped exactly this
; shape for unconfined_t ("Allow svirt_t connect to unconfined_t over a unix
; domain socket"), still live in 44.5. But it lets any confined VM reach any
; unconfined service socket on the host, and it was only ever the cheaper option
; because the cost of the alternative was unmeasured. It was then measured: the
; domain below is ordinary rules, no user-namespace machinery, because
; generate_virtiofsd_service already passes --sandbox=chroot. (Contrast
; ../../security/pasta_sandbox.cil, which exists precisely because pasta's
; userns+pivot_root sandbox needs cap_userns and tmpfs_t:dir mounton. Switching
; the sidecar to --sandbox=namespace would reopen that cost.)
;
; A dedicated type also separates virtiofsd from QEMU directly, so MCS
; categories stay unused (ADR 006 §9.5) — under the blanket allow, every VM's
; sidecar would share one type and only categories could tell them apart.
;
; WHY THIS IS HOST-GLOBAL AND NOT A PER-WORKLOAD BUNDLE
;
; Nothing here is per-workload: one process type, one exec type, one entrypoint,
; and one permission granted to svirt_t. Shipping it through the per-workload
; `[security].selinux_policy` bundle would mean N identical copies racing
; semodule, gated on a flag that has nothing to do with whether virtiofs works.
; The RPM installs and loads it once. The bundle keeps its existing job, the
; per-workload QEMU delta.
;
; NOTE FOR DEPLOYED HOSTS: the policy store lives in /etc, which ostree
; 3-way-merges, so a `bootc upgrade` does NOT deliver a changed module to a
; machine whose store has local modifications (the same caveat the image's
; Containerfile records for its own CIL modules). `workloadctl diagnose` reports
; whether this module is loaded so the gap is visible rather than silent.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Declarations
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(type wlvfsd_t)
(typeattributeset domain (wlvfsd_t))
(roletype system_r wlvfsd_t)

(type wlvfsd_exec_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))
(typeattributeset exec_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))
(typeattributeset entry_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))

; Retyping the binary is what keeps the entrypoint narrow. virtiofsd is
; unlabelled (bin_t), so a domain that took bin_t as its entrypoint would make
; *every* bin_t binary an entry into wlvfsd_t. This is the same shape the
; shipped policy already uses for swtpm and passt — and, one directory over, for
; QEMU itself: /usr/libexec/qemu-kvm is qemu_exec_t, not bin_t.
;
; A filecon is safe here specifically. file_contexts.local outranks the base
; file_contexts wholesale, so most-specific-wins applies only within one source
; — which is why the per-workload svirt_image_t rule must go through semanage
; and cannot ship as a filecon. Nothing registers /usr/libexec in
; file_contexts.local, so this rule competes with the base /usr/libexec/.*
; entry within one source, where specificity does apply.
(filecon "/usr/libexec/virtiofsd" file (system_u object_r wlvfsd_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Entry: a type transition on the retyped binary, no wrapper
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The sidecar needs none of the runcon machinery the QEMU unit uses, because it
; has a real exec to transition on rather than a Python wrapper.
(typetransition init_t wlvfsd_exec_t process wlvfsd_t)
(allow init_t wlvfsd_exec_t (file (getattr open read execute map)))
(allow init_t wlvfsd_t (process (transition)))
(allow wlvfsd_t wlvfsd_exec_t (file (entrypoint execute map getattr ioctl open read)))
(allow wlvfsd_t init_t (fd (use)))
(allow wlvfsd_t init_t (fifo_file (append getattr ioctl lock read write)))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What virtiofsd actually does
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Harvested permissive on Fedora 44. Every rule is ordinary: read the shared
; directory, create the socket, sd_notify back to systemd, NSS lookup for
; --socket-group, chroot.

; chown: root virtiofsd applies guest-requested ownership on create/chown, which
; is the whole reason the unit runs as root (see generate_virtiofsd_service).
; sys_chroot is --sandbox=chroot. There is deliberately no cap_userns or
; mounton here; if those appear, the sandbox mode changed.
(allow wlvfsd_t self (capability (chown dac_read_search setpcap sys_chroot sys_resource)))
(allow wlvfsd_t self (process (setcap setrlimit)))

; The shared dir lives under /var/lib/workloads/<name>/, which a VM workload's
; per-workload fcontext rule labels svirt_image_t at enable.
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (dir (getattr open read search)))

; /run/workload-vm(/.*)? is svirt_var_run_t — an alias of qemu_var_run_t, which
; CIL resolves to. Registered by the RPM's %post, not per workload.
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (dir (add_name getattr search write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (file (getattr lock open write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (sock_file (create setattr)))

; sd_notify, and the NSS lookups --socket-group=<user> needs.
(allow wlvfsd_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto ioctl)))
(allow wlvfsd_t systemd_userdbd_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))
(allow wlvfsd_t systemd_homed_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))
(allow wlvfsd_t systemd_userdbd_runtime_t (dir (getattr open read search)))
(allow wlvfsd_t systemd_userdbd_runtime_t (lnk_file (read)))
(allow wlvfsd_t systemd_userdbd_runtime_t (sock_file (write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t passwd_file_t (file (getattr open read)))
(allow wlvfsd_t init_var_run_t (dir (search)))

(allow wlvfsd_t sysctl_fs_t (dir (search)))
(allow wlvfsd_t sysctl_fs_t (file (getattr open read)))
(allow wlvfsd_t nsfs_t (file (getattr)))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The one line this module exists for
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Replaces the blanket `svirt_t -> unconfined_service_t` connectto with a grant
; scoped to exactly the daemon QEMU is supposed to be talking to.
(allow svirt_t wlvfsd_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))
